WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.360
Few things are as important to American
prosperity as how our schools fare

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.640
when it comes to teaching science,
technology, and math. Our scores on this

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:19.740
count are middling at best. Meanwhile, a
major international analysis recently

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:25.300
reported on STEM practices across dozens of
nations, including many of our leading competitors.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.740
How did the US stack up?
American students do more math homework

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.840
than their peers abroad. In 2015,
seventy-five percent of US eighth-graders

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.579
had homework at least twice a week.
That was 20 points higher than the

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:43.140
OECD average. US fourth-graders observed
science experiments about as frequently

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.430
as their peers in the typical OECD
nation, and American students use

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:52.500
computers in math class far more than
do their peers overseas. In 2015,

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.220
four in five US fourth-graders frequently
used computers. More homework,

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:01.280
plenty of experiments, more computers,
but middling scores.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:07.300
More of the same probably isn't the answer.
We just may need a new playbook.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.640
How do you think US STEM education
stacks up internationally?

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.450
Let us know in our poll.
Also, let us know what other topics you'd

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:20.610
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:24.439
more research and videos from AEI.

